naomandespam@terra.com.br (Ricardo Staudt) writes:

> Considering the good response from my last post ("A few hopefully good
> suggestions") I'm starting this thread to discuss and raise
> suggestions the next NH versions. Fell free to add your own
>  
> - Kick small monsters to throw them away (as in the last message)
> The kick has never been really useful in combat, I suggest that
> kicking a small monster would have the same result as kicking an light
> object (throwing them away), I think this is a great way to avoid
> (cock/chick)atrices when you have boots as well as escape/avoid
> annoying monsters when needed by keeping distance.

I second this idea, in particular for monks, although one has to 
debate what happens in the case of 

a) kicking a monster back into another monster (i.e., the kicked 
   monster would come to rest on an occupied square).  What should 
   happen?  My suggestion is that it mereley comes to rest one 
   square before monster 2, and produces a message like "The newt 
   flies back from your powerful kick!  The newt bumps into the gray 
   dragon!  The newt bounces back!", but of course some care is needed 
   to prevent silly messages for some combinations ("bounces back" 
   surely is a bad idea).  

b) kicking a monster into a trap/water/lava square.  Is the natural 
   assumption (getting trapped/drowned/burned/molten) a good 
   idea and save to abuse?  

c) kicking a monster against a door.  Would it be fun to assume 
   that this might break the door?  

> - Takedown/fall down
> This might also be interesting, by kick, by attacking or throwing
> something heavy (like a corpse), the opponent may loose his balance
> fall to the ground, becoming exposed to your attack for one or two
> turns. This would add some balance (or call it even more advantage) to
> big creatures vs smaller creatures, for example: A troll hitting a
> rogue with a war hammer taking the rogue down. *or* A rogue taking a
> troll down after attacking him from behind. Of course, this would only
> work on biped creatures.

This idea has the potential to make late game a little more interesting.  
If a powerful hit from a large creature could knock you down, making  
you easier to hit, Gehennom would get more exciting.  Additionally, I 
suggest that there should be a chance to lose your weapon, increased 
for #twoweaponing, making #twoweapon a bit less attractive.  

> - Caring a notepad
> I believe this may have already been suggested, but in any case, a
> notepad (starting with it or finding it) would be great for you to
> make your own notes during the game and reading them anytime, also,
> you could be able to view it after dying.

This would be quite some work to encode, I believe.  On a non--graphical 
system, it would be tedious to scroll through your notes until you 
find what you've been looking for, so if one implemented that, I'd 
favor a 'grep' option for that as well.

> - Improved #chat with certain people for certain actions
> An altar priest may charge (a considerably high tax) you to
> uncurse/bless one of your items. 

That could be a game balance issue in earlier game.  Later, 
you'll have tons of holy water and little desire to climb 
back to the priest, so almost no-one would use this, I 
would guess.  

